{"page_content": "5.4\nFLEX-2 Test Matrix\nFLEX-2\nSRD Rev. 1\n2. Allow, at a minimum, approximately two minutes for the ambient gas to reach quiescence.\n3. Energize the diagnostic devices and start the recording process and ensure that there is a\nlive down-link of the color camera\n4. Select the droplet\u2019s initial size and deploy the droplet (or droplets) in either a free-\ufb02oated\nor tethered mode.\n5. Wait few seconds for droplet deployment induced velocities to die down.\n6. Ignite and observe the combustion process.\n7. If the chamber environment, following a combustion event, permits a subsequent run then\nrepeat steps 3 through 6; if not, then start from step 1.\n5.4\nFLEX-2 Test Matrix\nA detailed test matrix is currently under development and will need to strike a balance between\nmeeting all the stated science objectives and the resource and operational constraints imposed\nby the \ufb02ight hardware and space station operations. As is customary, the exact test matrix\nand/or the sequence of test points will potentially change during the conduct of the FLEX-2\nexperiment. The extensive ground testing in the drop towers at the NASA Glenn Research\nCenter and the results from the earlier FLEX experiments will provide information which will\nbe used in \ufb01nalizing the \ufb01nal test matrix and test point sequencing. Nonetheless, since it is\nnecessary for the engineering team to have a preliminary test matrix envelope, in order to\nestimate consumables and prepare for the necessary safety reviews, a test matrix envelop has\nbeen presented in Section 4.3. A \ufb01nal test matrix, with individual test point speci\ufb01city, will be\nincorporated by addendum to this document in the the Appendix.\n46", "metadata": {"source": "FLEX-2 SRD Final.pdf", "page": 52}}